{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 27, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "**Early Life and Family Background**\nRaymond III was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Helvis of Ramla He was a member of the House of Saint-Germain which ruled the County of Tripoli in the Kingdom of Jerusalem He succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1153\n\nRaymond III was related to the ruling families of the other Christian states in Outremer through his mother who was a member of the House of Ibelin His family ties played an important role in his political decisions He was also a cousin of King Amalric I of Jerusalem\n\n**Marriage and Lack of Heirs**\nRaymond III married Agnes of Courtenay a sister of King Amalric I However their marriage was childless which led to a succession crisis in the County of Tripoli After his death the county was inherited by his nephew Raymond IV Count of Tripoli\n\nRaymond III's marriage to Agnes was likely arranged to strengthen ties between the County of Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusalem The marriage alliance proved important in his later political dealings\n\n**Military Campaigns and Politics**\nRaymond III participated in several military campaigns against the Muslim forces He was captured by Nur ad-Din Zangi in 1163 during the Battle of Inab He was released in 1164 after paying a large ransom\n\nRaymond III played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem He supported the claims of Humphrey IV of Toron to the regency of the kingdom during the minority of King Baldwin V He also opposed the marriage of Agnes of Courtenay to Hugh IV of Le Mans\n\n*highlighted section part 1* Raymond III was a skilled military leader and led the Christian forces to several victories against the Muslim armies\nHe also strengthened the defenses of the County of Tripoli by building new fortifications and restoring old ones\n\nRaymond III died in 1183 and was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV He was remembered as a brave and wise ruler who protected the County of Tripoli from Muslim invasions\n\n*highlighted section part 2* The County of Tripoli flourished during Raymond III's reign despite the constant threat from Muslim forces\nHe encouraged trade and commerce in the county which made it one of the wealthiest Christian states in Outremer\n\nRaymond III's death led to a succession crisis in the County of Tripoli However his nephew Raymond IV proved to be a capable ruler\n\n*highlighted section part 3* Raymond III's legacy was that of a strong and capable ruler who defended the County of Tripoli against all odds\nHe is still remembered as one of the most important rulers of the County of Tripoli during the Crusader period\n\nRaymond III's reign marked the beginning of the end of the Christian rule in Outremer The County of Tripoli fell to the Muslims in 1289 \n\nNote: The original text had commas which have been removed as per the user's request. This may affect the readability of the text in some areas.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -3.0179295539855957}, {"token": "Early", "logprob": -0.6469250321388245}, {"token": " Life", "logprob": -0.00021491125517059118}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.479900062084198}, {"token": " Family", "logprob": -3.177687406539917}, {"token": " Background", "logprob": -4.8329548835754395}, {"token": "**\n", "logprob": -0.06095476448535919}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.003715874394401908}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.5510462364763953e-05}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.691245941212401e-05}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.7040596604347229}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.5029812455177307}, {"token": " son", "logprob": -0.06974535435438156}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -2.396077979938127e-05}, {"token": " Raymond", "logprob": -0.1922169178724289}, {"token": " II", "logprob": -0.002449371386319399}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.15335465967655182}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.7523612768854946